\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The Clifford anticommutator}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The anticommutator in this proposition is the scalar
#{\iota_q(u)\iota_q(v)+\iota_q(v)\iota_q(u)=B_q(u,v)1}.
Mathematical physics writes that relation with an identity matrix.
When #{2} is invertible, the same relation is #{2b_q(u,v)1}.
For the even Clifford algebra, when the ground ring is a
noetherian domain, the module is projective of finite rank,
and the value module is invertible,
\citet{22.2.7 and (22.2.8), p. 346}{voight2021quaternionv107u}
states that the sum of the two ordered tensors of a pair,
each tensored with a linear form on the value module, equals
that linear form applied to the polar form of the pair.
The polar form is #{B_q} when the value module is the ground
ring. In that case, and when the linear form is the identity,
the identity is the scalar anticommutator above. That
identification is not made for a general value module.
Outside those hypotheses, the argument of \ref{fdip-000P}
stands.}
